{"id":"gen-1790979198-WKeDsWRmy1NlU9cGaEGL","object":"chat.completion","created":1790979198,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length","native_finish_reason":"max_tokens","message":{"role":"assistant","content":null,"refusal":null,"reasoning":"We have a CSP with variables a..p, domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints for pairs of variables (c1..c16) defined by allowed pairs. Additionally, there are \"bridges\" constraints c17..c20 that link variables across components: c17 is between c1 and c2 variables b,e; c18 between c2 and c3 variables f,i; c19 between c3 and c4 variables j,m; c20 between c4 and c1 variables n,a.\n\nWe need to \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c4, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nSo we need to find all assignments (m,n,o,p) that satisfy all constraints given the whole CSP. That includes constraints c13..c16 that involve m,n,o,p pairs directly, plus bridge constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). Also constraints c1..c12 involve other variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l. So we need to find all possible assignments for (m,n,o,p) that can be extended to a full solution for the entire CSP. So effectively we need to compute the projection of the solution set onto variables m,n,o,p (c4) given all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for component c4, i.e., all possible (m,n,o,p) that belong to some full solution of the CSP. Then output them sorted lexicographically (presumably by m,n,o,p in that order). The output must be JSON with the envelope format, but only editing the payload.claim.value.tuples part.\n\nThus we need to compute the solution set for the entire CSP, then extract the projection onto (m,n,o,p). The constraints are finite; domain size is 4; there are 16 variables, each with domain size 4. The constraints are binary constraints (allowed pairs). So we could brute force search: 4^16 = 4,294,967,296 possibilities, which is large but maybe manageable with pruning? However we can also reason logically.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible (m,n,o,p) that can be part of a full solution. It might be a relatively small set.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's try to approach systematically.\n\nWe have components:\n\nc1: a,b,c,d\nc2: e,f,g,h\nc3: i,j,k,l\nc4: m,n,o,p\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs:\n[\n[3,2],\n[2,3],\n[3,1],\n[0,2],\n[1,1],\n[0,1]\n]\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed:\n[\n[2,1],\n[0,2],\n[3,2],\n[0,0],\n[3,1],\n[0,3]\n]\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed:\n[\n[1,2],\n[2,2],\n[2,1],\n[3,2],\n[0,1],\n[0,0]\n]\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed:\n[\n[2,3],\n[2,2],\n[3,0],\n[1,3],\n[3,3],\n[0,1]\n]\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed:\n[\n[0,1],\n[1,1],\n[2,2],\n[0,2],\n[3,3],\n[2,1]\n]\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed:\n[\n[1,3],\n[2,3],\n[0,1],\n[2,0],\n[1,1],\n[1,0]\n]\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed:\n[\n[3,2],\n[3,1],\n[2,1],\n[1,0],\n[0,0],\n[3,0]\n]\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[1,1],\n[2,2],\n[2,1],\n[0,3],\n[1,2]\n]\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed:\n[\n[0,3],\n[0,2],\n[0,0],\n[1,1],\n[3,2],\n[2,3]\n]\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed:\n[\n[3,0],\n[0,2],\n[1,0],\n[0,1],\n[1,3],\n[3,2]\n]\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed:\n[\n[0,2],\n[1,3],\n[3,3],\n[2,0],\n[3,2],\n[1,0]\n]\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[1,1],\n[3,3],\n[2,2],\n[3,1],\n[0,0]\n]\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed:\n[\n[0,1],\n[2,2],\n[0,3],\n[3,0],\n[2,1],\n[2,0]\n]\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed:\n[\n[1,3],\n[3,2],\n[3,0],\n[0,1],\n[2,0],\n[0,3]\n]\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed:\n[\n[3,2],\n[0,2],\n[3,3],\n[0,1],\n[2,1],\n[1,1]\n]\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[3,3],\n[0,0],\n[1,2],\n[1,3],\n[2,3]\n]\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[0,2]\n]\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed:\n[\n[1,0],\n[2,1]\n]\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed:\n[\n[3,0],\n[0,0]\n]\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed:\n[\n[1,3],\n[1,0]\n]\n\nSo we have a CSP with 16 variables, binary constraints.\n\nWe need to find all possible assignments for (m,n,o,p). Approach: solve the CSP systematically, perhaps using constraint propagation or backtracking manually. Since domain size is small, we can attempt to enumerate possibilities for each component and combine.\n\nWe can treat each component as a chain: c1 (a,b,c,d) has constraints c1 (a,b), c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). That's a cycle: a->b->c->d->a. So it's a CSP on 4 variables with constraints forming a cycle.\n\nc2 (e,f,g,h) has constraints c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) - also a cycle.\n\nc3 (i,j,k,l) has constraints c9 (i,j), c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i) - also a cycle.\n\nc4 (m,n,o,p) has constraints c13 (m,n), c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m) - also a cycle.\n\nAdditionally, there are cross-component bridges: c17 (b,e), c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m), c20 (n,a). So we have connections linking the cycles.\n\nThus the whole CSP is a graph of 16 variables with cycles plus cross edges.\n\nWe need to find all solutions.\n\nWe could attempt to solve using backtracking manually. But there are many possibilities. However, maybe the constraints are restrictive enough that the number of solutions is small.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions for c4 (m,n,o,p). There may be many, but maybe not too many.\n\nLet's try to systematically solve.\n\nWe have 4 variables per component. Let's denote:\n\nComponent c1: a,b,c,d\nc2: e,f,g,h\nc3: i,j,k,l\nc4: m,n,o,p\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) from list A1\nc2: allowed (b,c) from list A2\nc3: allowed (c,d) from list A3\nc4: allowed (d,a) from list A4\n\nc5: allowed (e,f) from list A5\nc6: allowed (f,g) from list A6\nc7: allowed (g,h) from list A7\nc8: allowed (h,e) from list A8\n\nc9: allowed (i,j) from list A9\nc10: allowed (j,k) from list A10\nc11: allowed (k,l) from list A11\nc12: allowed (l,i) from list A12\n\nc13: allowed (m,n) from list A13\nc14: allowed (n,o) from list A14\nc15: allowed (o,p) from list A15\nc16: allowed (p,m) from list A16\n\nc17: allowed (b,e) from list A17\nc18: allowed (f,i) from list A18\nc19: allowed (j,m) from list A19\nc20: allowed (n,a) from list A20\n\nAll variables domain = {0,1,2,3}.\n\nGoal: find all assignments for (m,n,o,p) that can be extended to a full solution.\n\nWe need to consider constraints linking components: c17 links b and e; c18 links f and i; c19 links j and m; c20 links n and a.\n\nThus the cycles of each component are linked via these cross constraints, forming a larger structure.\n\nWe could try to solve the entire CSP using a systematic approach: treat the graph as having cycles; we could try to enumerate possibilities for one component at a time, propagate constraints.\n\nGiven the small domain, we could attempt to manually enumerate possibilities for each component using backtracking, perhaps using reasoning to prune.\n\nWe need all solutions for c4, so we might focus on solving for (m,n,o,p) while also considering constraints linking to other components, but we could also solve the entire CSP and then extract (m,n,o,p). The number of solutions might be manageable.\n\nWe need to be careful to enumerate all solutions. Let's try to systematically explore using constraint propagation.\n\nApproach: Since each component is a cycle of 4 variables, we can solve each component in isolation given the values of some variables. For example, c1 can be solved given a (or b). But there are cross constraints linking a (c20) to n, and b (c17) to e, etc. So we need to consider interactions.\n\nWe could treat the entire graph as a constraint satisfaction problem and attempt to enumerate all solutions using backtracking with pruning. Doing this manually is tedious, but we can try to reduce the search space by analyzing constraints.\n\nFirst, let's list the allowed pairs for each constraint for reference:\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed:\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n(3,1)\n(0,2)\n(1,1)\n(0,1)\n\nc2 (b,c):\n(2,1)\n(0,2)\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(3,1)\n(0,3)\n\nc3 (c,d):\n(1,2)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(3,2)\n(0,1)\n(0,0)\n\nc4 (d,a):\n(2,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,0)\n(1,3)\n(3,3)\n(0,1)\n\nc5 (e,f):\n(0,1)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(0,2)\n(3,3)\n(2,1)\n\nc6 (f,g):\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(1,0)\n\nc7 (g,h):\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,0)\n(0,0)\n(3,0)\n\nc8 (h,e):\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(0,3)\n(1,2)\n\nc9 (i,j):\n(0,3)\n(0,2)\n(0,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n\nc10 (j,k):\n(3,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(3,2)\n\nc11 (k,l):\n(0,2)\n(1,3)\n(3,3)\n(2,0)\n(3,2)\n(1,0)\n\nc12 (l,i):\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,1)\n(0,0)\n\nc13 (m,n):\n(0,1)\n(2,2)\n(0,3)\n(3,0)\n(2,1)\n(2,0)\n\nc14 (n,o):\n(1,3)\n(3,2)\n(3,0)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n\nc15 (o,p):\n(3,2)\n(0,2)\n(3,3)\n(0,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,1)\n\nc16 (p,m):\n(2,0)\n(3,3)\n(0,0)\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n\nc17 (b,e):\n(2,0)\n(0,2)\n\nc18 (f,i):\n(1,0)\n(2,1)\n\nc19 (j,m):\n(3,0)\n(0,0)\n\nc20 (n,a):\n(1,3)\n(1,0)\n\nThus cross constraints are very restrictive: c17 only allows (b,e) = (2,0) or (0,2). c18 only allows (f,i) = (1,0) or (2,1). c19 only allows (j,m) = (3,0) or (0,0). c20 only allows (n,a) = (1,3) or (1,0).\n\nThus these cross constraints strongly restrict values of b, e; f,i; j,m; n,a.\n\nWe also have constraints c13..c16 linking m,n,o,p, and cross constraints c19 and c20 linking m to j and n to a. So m and n are heavily constrained.\n\nThus we can start by enumerating possibilities for cross constraints.\n\nLet’s list possibilities for each cross constraint:\n\nc17 (b,e):\n- Option 1: b=2, e=0\n- Option 2: b=0, e=2\n\nc18 (f,i):\n- Option A: f=1, i=0\n- Option B: f=2, i=1\n\nc19 (j,m):\n- Option α: j=3, m=0\n- Option β: j=0, m=0\n\nc20 (n,a):\n- Option X: n=1, a=3\n- Option Y: n=1, a=0\n\nThus we have 2*2*2*2 = 16 combinations of these cross constraints.\n\nNow we need to consider constraints within each component that involve these variables.\n\nComponent c1: variables a,b,c,d. Constraints: c1 (a,b), c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). Also cross constraints: c17 (b,e) and c20 (n,a). So a appears in c1 and c4 and c20; b appears in c1, c2, c17.\n\nComponent c2: variables e,f,g,h. Constraints: c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e). Cross constraints: c17 (b,e) and c18 (f,i). So e appears in c5, c8, c17; f appears in c5, c6, c18.\n\nComponent c3: variables i,j,k,l. Constraints: c9 (i,j), c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i). Cross constraints: c18 (f,i) and c19 (j,m). So i appears in c9, c12, c18; j appears in c9, c10, c19.\n\nComponent c4: variables m,n,o,p. Constraints: c13 (m,n), c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m). Cross constraints: c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). So m appears in c13, c16, c19; n appears in c13, c14, c20.\n\nThus the cross constraints tie the cycles together.\n\nWe need to find all solutions for the whole CSP. Let's attempt to solve using a systematic approach: we can treat the cross constraints as givens, then propagate within each component.\n\nGiven the cross constraints, we have values for some variables: b,e; f,i; j,m; n,a.\n\nThus we can treat those as known values for each combination, then solve each component's remaining variables.\n\nBut note that some of these variables also appear in other cross constraints: e appears only in c17 and c5,c8; b appears only in c17 and c1,c2; f appears only in c18 and c5,c6; i appears only in c18 and c9,c12; j appears only in c19 and c9,c10; m appears only in c19 and c13,c16; n appears only in c20 and c13,c14; a appears only in c20 and c1,c4.\n\nThus each variable appears in exactly one cross constraint (except maybe a appears only in c20 and c1,c4; b appears only in c17 and c1,c2; etc). So cross constraints essentially fix the values of those variables (b,e; f,i; j,m; n,a) to specific pairs.\n\nThus we can treat each cross constraint as a relation that restricts the values of those two variables.\n\nThus we need to consider all 16 combos of these cross constraints, then for each, solve the component cycles.\n\nWe need to find all solutions for the entire CSP. Then we extract (m,n,o,p) values.\n\nWe can attempt to enumerate all possibilities for (b,e) from c17, (f,i) from c18, (j,m) from c19, (n,a) from c20. Then for each combination, we need to find assignments for the remaining variables in each component: for c1: a,b,c,d; we know a from c20 and b from c17. So a and b are known. Then we need to find c,d such that constraints c2 (b,c) and c3 (c,d) and c4 (d,a) are satisfied. So given a and b, we need to find c,d.\n\nSimilarly for c2: e,f,g,h; we know e from c17 and f from c18. Then we need to find g,h satisfying c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e). So given e,f, find g,h.\n\nc3: i,j,k,l; we know i from c18 and j from c19. Then need to find k,l satisfying c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i). So given i,j, find k,l.\n\nc4: m,n,o,p; we know m from c19 and n from c20. Then need to find o,p satisfying c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m). So given m,n, find o,p.\n\nThus each component reduces to a smaller CSP: given two variables (the ones from cross constraints), find the remaining two variables.\n\nThus the total number of solutions is product of number of solutions for each component (if independent). However, there might be interactions? The cross constraints are independent across components: c17 ties b and e (c1 and c2), c18 ties f and i (c2 and c3), c19 ties j and m (c3 and c4), c20 ties n and a (c4 and c1). So there is a chain linking the components: c1-c2-c3-c4-c1. So the components are not independent because the cross constraints create connections across components. But we have already accounted for that: we fix values for b,e; f,i; j,m; n,a. Those are the cross constraints. The components themselves are independent given those values because each component's constraints only involve its own variables plus the cross constraints which we have fixed. There's no further cross constraints linking components beyond those four pairs. So after fixing those values, the components become independent: we can solve c1 given a,b; c2 given e,f; c3 given i,j; c4 given m,n. There is no further coupling.\n\nThus the total number of solutions is the product of the number of solutions for each component for each combination of cross constraint values.\n\nThus we can enumerate all combos of cross constraints (16 combos), and for each, count the number of solutions for each component. Then the total number of solutions is sum over combos of product of component solution counts.\n\nBut we only need the projection onto (m,n,o,p). So we need to collect all possible (m,n,o,p) values that appear in any solution.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all combos of cross constraints, and for each, enumerate all solutions for each component, then record (m,n,o,p) values.\n\nThus we need to solve each component subproblem for each possible pair values.\n\nThus we need to solve:\n\n- Subproblem C1: Given a and b (both from cross constraints), find all (c,d) such that constraints c2 (b,c) and c3 (c,d) and c4 (d,a) hold.\n\n- Subproblem C2: Given e and f, find all (g,h) such that constraints c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) hold.\n\n- Subproblem C3: Given i and j, find all (k,l) such that constraints c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i) hold.\n\n- Subproblem C4: Given m and n, find all (o,p) such that constraints c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m) hold.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possibilities for each subproblem given the specific values.\n\nThus we need to generate all possible assignments for a,b from cross constraints c20 and c17. Let's list possible combos:\n\nc20 (n,a): (n=1,a=3) or (n=1,a=0). So a ∈ {3,0}, n=1 always? Wait both options have n=1. Yes both have n=1. So n is fixed to 1 for all solutions. Interesting: c20 only allows n=1. So n=1 always. Then a is either 3 or 0.\n\nc17 (b,e): (b=2,e=0) or (b=0,e=2). So b ∈ {2,0}, e ∈ {0,2} correspondingly.\n\nc18 (f,i): (f=1,i=0) or (f=2,i=1). So f ∈ {1,2}, i ∈ {0,1} correspondingly.\n\nc19 (j,m): (j=3,m=0) or (j=0,m=0). So m=0 always! Both options have m=0. So m is fixed to 0. Then j is either 3 or 0.\n\nThus we have:\n\n- n = 1 always.\n- a ∈ {3,0}\n- b ∈ {2,0} (with e accordingly)\n- e = 0 if b=2 else e=2 if b=0 (since c17 pairs: (2,0) or (0,2))\n- f ∈ {1,2} (with i accordingly)\n- i = 0 if f=1 else i=1 if f=2\n- m = 0 always.\n- j ∈ {3,0} (depending on which option: if j=3,m=0; if j=0,m=0)\n- So j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus the cross constraints reduce the degrees of freedom significantly: we have choices for a (2 possibilities), b (2 possibilities), f (2 possibilities), j (2 possibilities). So total combos = 2*2*2*2 = 16 as expected.\n\nThus we need to consider all combos of (a ∈ {3,0}, b ∈ {2,0} with e accordingly, f ∈ {1,2} with i accordingly, j ∈ {3,0} with m=0). So we can enumerate all 16 combos.\n\nNow for each combo, we need to solve the component subproblems:\n\nC1: given a and b, find c,d.\n\nC2: given e and f, find g,h.\n\nC3: given i and j, find k,l.\n\nC4: given m=0 and n=1, find o,p.\n\nThus we need to enumerate solutions for each subproblem given the specific values.\n\nThus we can treat each subproblem as a small CSP with 2 variables (c,d) etc.\n\nWe can manually enumerate possibilities for each subproblem.\n\nLet's start with C4 because it's independent of many choices: given m=0, n=1, find o,p satisfying constraints c14 (n,o) i.e., (1,o) must be in allowed pairs for c14; c15 (o,p) must be allowed; c16 (p,m) i.e., (p,0) must be allowed.\n\nThus C4 is independent of other choices (since m and n are fixed). So we can solve C4 once: find all (o,p) such that:\n\n- (n=1, o) ∈ allowed pairs for c14 (n,o) => allowed pairs for c14 are: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So we need (1, o) to be one of those. So we need o such that (1,o) is in that list.\n\nList allowed (n,o) pairs: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So the first component is n; second is o. So we need n=1, so we need a pair where first entry is 1. Among the allowed pairs, only (1,3) has first entry 1. So (n=1, o=3) is allowed. No other pair has n=1. So o must be 3.\n\nThus o = 3 is forced.\n\nNow given o=3, we need to satisfy c15 (o,p): allowed pairs for c15 are: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So we need (o=3, p) to be one of these. So p must satisfy (3,p) ∈ allowed. The allowed pairs with first entry 3 are: (3,2), (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus p can be 2 or 3.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c16 (p,m): allowed pairs for c16 are: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So we need (p,m) = (p,0) to be allowed. So we need a pair where second entry is 0. Among allowed pairs, those with second entry 0 are: (2,0), (0,0). Also maybe others? Let's list all:\n\nc16 allowed pairs:\n- (2,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (2,3)\n\nThus only (2,0) and (0,0) have second entry 0. So p must be either 2 or 0.\n\nThus p ∈ {2,0}.\n\nCombine with p ∈ {2,3} from previous step, we get p must be 2 (common). So p=2.\n\nThus the only solution for C4 is (o=3, p=2). So (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2). So there is exactly one tuple for component c4 across all solutions! That seems plausible. But we must double-check if there are any other possibilities: Did we consider all allowed pairs for c14? Yes. Did we miss any pair where n=1? Only (1,3). So o forced to 3. Then p forced to 2. So indeed unique.\n\nThus the projection onto c4 is a single tuple: (m=0, n=1, o=3, p=2). So answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify that this tuple indeed satisfies all constraints, including cross constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). Since m=0, c19 allows (j,m) = (j,0) must be either (3,0) or (0,0). So j can be 3 or 0. So both are allowed. So j is free (3 or 0). Similarly, n=1, c20 allows (n,a) = (1,a) must be either (1,3) or (1,0). So a can be 3 or 0. So a is free (3 or 0). So indeed (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2) is consistent with cross constraints.\n\nThus the answer for c4 is a single tuple.\n\nBut we must ensure that there are no other solutions for c4 that could arise if we consider other combos of cross constraints? But we saw that m and n are fixed by cross constraints: m=0 always, n=1 always. So any solution must have those values. Then we solved C4 subproblem for those values and found a unique solution (o=3,p=2). So indeed there is exactly one possible tuple for c4.\n\nThus the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify that the constraints for c4 (c13..c16) are satisfied for (m=0,n=1,o=3,p=2). Let's check:\n\nc13 (m,n) = (0,1) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for c13: (0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0). So (0,1) is allowed. Good.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (1,3) allowed? Allowed pairs for c14: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So (1,3) is allowed.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,2) allowed? Allowed pairs for c15: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So (3,2) is allowed.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (2,0) allowed? Allowed pairs for c16: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So (2,0) is allowed.\n\nThus c4 constraints satisfied.\n\nThus indeed (0,1,3,2) is a valid tuple for component c4.\n\nNow we need to verify that there are no other possible (o,p) combos for given (m=0,n=1). We solved C4 and found only (o=3,p=2). Let's double-check if there might be any alternative for o from c14: we only considered (1,3) because n=1. But is there any other allowed pair where first entry is 1? Let's list c14 allowed pairs again: \n\nc14 allowed pairs:\n[\n[1,3],\n[3,2],\n[3,0],\n[0,1],\n[2,0],\n[0,3]\n]\n\nThus first entries: 1,3,3,0,2,0. So only (1,3) has first entry 1. So o=3 forced.\n\nThus o is forced.\n\nNow for c15: allowed pairs list:\n[\n[3,2],\n[0,2],\n[3,3],\n[0,1],\n[2,1],\n[1,1]\n]\n\nThus for o=3, allowed p: (3,2) and (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c16: allowed pairs:\n[\n[2,0],\n[3,3],\n[0,0],\n[1,2],\n[1,3],\n[2,3]\n]\n\nThus for p=2, allowed m: (2,0) is allowed (since m=0). So p=2 works. For p=3, allowed m: (3,3) is allowed only if m=3, but m=0, so p=3 not allowed. Also (1,3) has m=3? No, (1,3) means (p,m) = (1,3) so p=1,m=3, not relevant. (2,3) means p=2,m=3, not relevant. So p=3 not allowed. So p must be 2. So indeed unique.\n\nThus C4 has exactly one solution.\n\nThus the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify that the other components can be solved for any combination of cross constraints (a,b,f,j). However, we don't need to enumerate those because we only need c4 tuples. However, we need to ensure that there is at least one full solution for each cross constraint combination that yields the c4 tuple. If some cross constraint combos lead to inconsistency in other components, then the c4 tuple may not be realizable. But we need to find all (m,n,o,p) that can be part of a full solution. Since c4 tuple is unique, we need to check if there exists at least one full solution consistent with that tuple. If there is at least one full solution, then that tuple is included. If there is none, then there are no solutions for c4 (but we found a solution for c4 constraints alone, but we need to ensure that other components can be satisfied with the cross constraints linking to m,n). So we need to check if there exists at least one assignment for the other variables (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l) that satisfies all constraints given the cross constraints and the c4 tuple.\n\nThus we need to find at least one full solution for the entire CSP that includes (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2). Since we have cross constraints linking m to j and n to a, we need to assign j and a accordingly: j ∈ {3,0} (since (j,m) = (j,0) must be either (3,0) or (0,0)). So j can be 3 or 0. Similarly, a ∈ {3,0} (since (n,a) = (1,a) must be either (1,3) or (1,0)). So a can be 3 or 0.\n\nThus we have two choices for j and two choices for a. Combined with choices for b (2) and f (2). So total 16 combos.\n\nNow we need to see if for each combination we can find assignments for the remaining variables in each component (c1,c2,c3,c4). Since c4 is fixed, we need to solve c1 given a,b; c2 given e,f; c3 given i,j; c4 already solved.\n\nThus we need to check if for each combination of a,b,e,f,i,j (subject to cross constraints), there exist solutions for c1,c2,c3. If any combination yields no solution for at least one component, then that combination is invalid, but the c4 tuple may still be realizable via other combos. So we need to find at least one combo that yields a full solution. If there is at least one, then the tuple is included.\n\nThus we need to check existence of solutions for c1,c2,c3 for some combos.\n\nThus we need to solve subproblems:\n\nC1: given a ∈ {3,0} and b ∈ {2,0} (with e accordingly: e = 0 if b=2 else e=2 if b=0), find c,d such that constraints c2 (b,c) and c3 (c,d) and c4 (d,a) hold.\n\nC2: given e ∈ {0,2} and f ∈ {1,2} (with i accordingly: i = 0 if f=1 else i=1 if f=2), find g,h such that constraints c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) hold.\n\nC3: given i ∈ {0,1} and j ∈ {3,0} (with m=0 fixed), find k,l such that constraints c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i) hold.\n\nThus we need to find at least one combination of (a,b,f,j) that yields solutions for all three subproblems.\n\nThus we need to enumerate possibilities for each subproblem.\n\nGiven the small domain, we can manually enumerate possibilities for each subproblem.\n\nLet's start with C3 (i,j) subproblem. This is independent of other components. So we can enumerate all possible (i,j) pairs from cross constraints: i ∈ {0,1} (depending on f), j ∈ {3,0} (depending on which option). So there are 2*2 = 4 combos for (i,j). For each, we need to find (k,l) satisfying c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i). Let's enumerate all possibilities for each (i,j).\n\nFirst, list allowed pairs for c10 (j,k): allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for each j, possible k values:\n\n- If j=3: allowed k such that (3,k) ∈ allowed. Pairs with j=3: (3,0) and (3,2). So k ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- If j=0: allowed k such that (0,k) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,2), (0,1). Also maybe (0,?) from other pairs? Let's list all c10 pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for j=0, we have (0,2) and (0,1). So k ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus for j=3, k ∈ {0,2}; for j=0, k ∈ {2,1}.\n\nNow c11 (k,l): allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So for each k, possible l values:\n\n- If k=0: allowed l such that (0,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,2). So l=2.\n\n- If k=1: allowed l such that (1,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 1: (1,3), (1,0). So l ∈ {3,0}.\n\n- If k=2: allowed l such that (2,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,0). So l=0.\n\n- If k=3: allowed l such that (3,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 3: (3,3), (3,2). So l ∈ {3,2}.\n\nThus for each k, l is determined (or limited).\n\nNow c12 (l,i): allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,2), (3,1), (0,0). So for each l, possible i values such that (l,i) ∈ allowed.\n\nThus we need to find assignments (k,l) such that for given i, (l,i) is allowed.\n\nThus we can enumerate all possibilities for each (i,j) combo.\n\nLet's enumerate all combos for (i,j):\n\nCase 1: i=0, j=3\nCase 2: i=0, j=0\nCase 3: i=1, j=3\nCase 4: i=1, j=0\n\nWe'll enumerate for each.\n\n### Case 1: i=0, j=3\n\nj=3 => k ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- Subcase k=0: then l must be 2 (from c11). Then we need (l,i) = (2,0) allowed? c12 allowed pairs include (2,0). Yes (2,0) is allowed. So (k=0,l=2) works.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l must be 0 (from c11). Then need (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? c12 includes (0,0). Yes. So (k=2,l=0) works.\n\nThus for (i=0,j=3), we have two solutions: (k,l) = (0,2) and (2,0).\n\nThus C3 has solutions for (i=0,j=3).\n\n### Case 2: i=0, j=0\n\nj=0 => k ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l=0 (c11). Need (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? Yes. So (k=2,l=0) works.\n\n- Subcase k=1: then l ∈ {3,0} (c11). Need (l,i) = (l,0) allowed? Check c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,2), (3,1), (0,0). So (l,0) allowed only if l=2? Actually (2,0) is allowed, (0,0) is allowed. So l must be 0 or 2. But for k=1, l ∈ {3,0}. So l=0 works (since (0,0) allowed). l=3 does not work because (3,0) is not in c12 allowed pairs (allowed pairs with l=3 are (3,3) and (3,1)). So only l=0 works.\n\nThus for (i=0,j=0), we have solutions: (k=2,l=0) and (k=1,l=0). So two solutions.\n\nThus C3 has solutions for (i=0,j=0).\n\n### Case 3: i=1, j=3\n\nj=3 => k ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- Subcase k=0: then l=2 (c11). Need (l,i) = (2,1) allowed? c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,2), (3,1), (0,0). (2,1) is not allowed. So k=0 fails.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l=0 (c11). Need (l,i) = (0,1) allowed? c12 allowed pairs: (0,0) only for l=0? Actually (0,0) is allowed, (0,?) others? Only (0,0) appears. So (0,1) not allowed. So k=2 fails.\n\nThus no solutions for (i=1,j=3). So C3 has no solutions for (i=1,j=3).\n\n### Case 4: i=1, j=0\n\nj=0 => k ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l=0 (c11). Need (l,i) = (0,1) allowed? No, (0,1) not allowed. So k=2 fails.\n\n- Subcase k=1: then l ∈ {3,0} (c11). Need (l,i) = (l,1) allowed? c12 allowed pairs: (1,1) is allowed (l=1,i=1). But l ∈ {3,0}. So (3,1) is allowed? Yes, c12 includes (3,1). So l=3 works: (3,1) allowed. Also (0,1) is not allowed. So l=3 works.\n\nThus for (i=1,j=0), we have solution (k=1,l=3). Also check if l=0 works? (0,1) not allowed. So only (k=1,l=3) works.\n\nThus C3 has solutions for (i=1,j=0) (one solution).\n\nThus summary of C3 solutions:\n\n- (i=0,j=3): (k,l) = (0,2) or (2,0) (2 solutions)\n- (i=0,j=0): (k,l) = (2,0) or (1,0) (2 solutions)\n- (i=1,j=3): none\n- (i=1,j=0): (k,l) = (1,3) (1 solution)\n\nThus for each possible (i,j) there are solutions except (i=1,j=3) which has none.\n\nThus the cross constraints for i and j must not be (i=1,j=3). That corresponds to f=2 (i=1) and j=3 (i.e., j=3). So if we choose f=2 (i=1) and j=3, then C3 has no solution, making that combination invalid. So we must avoid that combination.\n\nThus we have 4 combos for (i,j). Among them, 3 combos are viable (i=0,j=3; i=0,j=0; i=1,j=0). The combo (i=1,j=3) is invalid.\n\nThus the cross constraints must avoid f=2 and j=3 simultaneously. So either f=1 (i=0) or j=0 (or both). So we need to ensure at least one valid combination.\n\nNow we need to consider C2 (e,f) subproblem. e ∈ {0,2} depending on b; f ∈ {1,2} depending on choice. So we have 2*2 = 4 combos for (e,f). For each, we need to find (g,h) satisfying c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e). Let's enumerate all possibilities for each (e,f) pair.\n\nList allowed pairs for each constraint:\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed pairs:\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(1,0)\n\nThus for each f, possible g values:\n\n- If f=1: allowed g such that (1,g) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 1: (1,3), (1,1), (1,0). So g ∈ {3,1,0}.\n\n- If f=2: allowed g such that (2,g) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,3), (2,0). So g ∈ {3,0}.\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed pairs:\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,0)\n(0,0)\n(3,0)\n\nThus for each g, possible h values:\n\n- If g=0: allowed h such that (0,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,0). So h=0.\n\n- If g=1: allowed h such that (1,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 1: (1,0). So h=0.\n\n- If g=2: allowed h such that (2,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,1). So h=1.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h such that (3,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 3: (3,2), (3,1), (3,0). So h ∈ {2,1,0}.\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed pairs:\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(0,3)\n(1,2)\n\nThus for each h, possible e values such that (h,e) ∈ allowed.\n\nThus we need to find (g,h) such that:\n\n- g ∈ allowed for given f.\n- h ∈ allowed for given g.\n- (h,e) ∈ allowed for given e.\n\nThus we can enumerate for each (e,f) combo.\n\nList possible (e,f) combos based on cross constraints:\n\nc17: (b,e) pairs: (2,0) or (0,2). So e ∈ {0,2}. b ∈ {2,0} accordingly.\n\nc18: (f,i) pairs: (1,0) or (2,1). So f ∈ {1,2}. i accordingly.\n\nThus (e,f) combos: e=0 with f=1; e=0 with f=2; e=2 with f=1; e=2 with f=2. So four combos.\n\nThus we need to solve C2 for each of these four combos.\n\nLet's enumerate each case:\n\n#### Case A: e=0, f=1\n\n- f=1 => g ∈ {3,1,0}\n- For each g, find h:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=1 => h=0\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Then need (h,e) = (h,0) allowed? c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So (h,0) allowed only if h=2? Actually (2,0) is allowed. Also maybe (0,0) not allowed. So only h=2 yields (2,0). So h must be 2.\n\nThus we need h=2. So we need to find g such that h=2 is allowed.\n\n- g=3 => h can be 2,1,0. So h=2 possible. So g=3 works (with h=2).\n- g=1 => h=0 only, not 2. So g=1 fails.\n- g=0 => h=0 only, fails.\n\nThus only solution: g=3, h=2.\n\nThus for (e=0,f=1), we have a unique solution (g=3,h=2). Check constraints: c6 (f=1,g=3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is allowed. c7 (g=3,h=2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is allowed. c8 (h=2,e=0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is allowed. So valid.\n\nThus C2 has exactly one solution for (e=0,f=1).\n\n#### Case B: e=0, f=2\n\n- f=2 => g ∈ {3,0}\n- For each g:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Need (h,e) = (h,0) allowed? As before, only h=2 allowed (since (2,0) allowed). So h must be 2.\n\nThus we need h=2. So we need a g that allows h=2.\n\n- g=3 => h can be 2,1,0. So h=2 possible. So g=3 works (with h=2).\n- g=0 => h=0 only, fails.\n\nThus solution: g=3, h=2.\n\nThus for (e=0,f=2), we have solution (g=3,h=2). Check constraints: c6 (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is allowed. c7 (3,2) allowed? Yes. c8 (2,0) allowed? Yes. So valid.\n\nThus C2 has exactly one solution for (e=0,f=2).\n\n#### Case C: e=2, f=1\n\n- f=1 => g ∈ {3,1,0}\n- For each g:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=1 => h=0\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Need (h,e) = (h,2) allowed? c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So (h,2) allowed if h=1? Actually (1,2) is allowed. Also (2,2) is allowed (h=2). Also maybe (0,2) not allowed. (2,2) is allowed. (1,2) is allowed. So h ∈ {1,2} yields (h,2) allowed.\n\nThus we need h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow find g such that h can be 1 or 2.\n\n- g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}. So h can be 2 or 1. So g=3 works (choose h=2 or h=1). So there are two possible h values: h=2 or h=1.\n\n- g=1 => h=0 only, not allowed.\n\n- g=0 => h=0 only, not allowed.\n\nThus possible solutions: (g=3, h=2) or (g=3, h=1). Both satisfy constraints? Let's check each:\n\n- (g=3, h=2): c6 (1,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,2) allowed? Yes. c8 (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is allowed.\n\n- (g=3, h=1): c6 (1,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) allowed. c8 (1,2) allowed? Yes (1,2) allowed.\n\nThus both are valid. So C2 has two solutions for (e=2,f=1): (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1).\n\nThus count = 2.\n\n#### Case D: e=2, f=2\n\n- f=2 => g ∈ {3,0}\n- For each g:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Need (h,e) = (h,2) allowed? As before, h ∈ {1,2} allowed.\n\nThus we need h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nCheck possibilities:\n\n- g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}. So h can be 2 or 1. So g=3 works with h=2 or h=1.\n\n- g=0 => h=0 only, not allowed.\n\nThus solutions: (g=3, h=2) and (g=3, h=1). Both satisfy constraints? Check:\n\n- (g=3, h=2): c6 (2,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,2) allowed? Yes. c8 (2,2) allowed? Yes.\n\n- (g=3, h=1): c6 (2,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,1) allowed? Yes. c8 (1,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus C2 has two solutions for (e=2,f=2): (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1).\n\nThus summary for C2:\n\n- (e=0,f=1): 1 solution: (g=3,h=2)\n- (e=0,f=2): 1 solution: (g=3,h=2)\n- (e=2,f=1): 2 solutions: (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1)\n- (e=2,f=2): 2 solutions: (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1)\n\nThus total solutions for C2 across all combos: 1+1+2+2 = 6 solutions.\n\nNow we need to consider C1 (a,b) subproblem. a ∈ {3,0} (depending on cross constraint c20). b ∈ {2,0} (depending on cross constraint c17). So we have 2*2 = 4 combos for (a,b). For each, we need to find (c,d) satisfying constraints c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). Let's enumerate.\n\nList allowed pairs for each constraint:\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed pairs:\n(2,1)\n(0,2)\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(3,1)\n(0,3)\n\nThus for each b, possible c values:\n\n- If b=2: allowed c such that (2,c) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,1). So c=1.\n\n- If b=0: allowed c such that (0,c) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,2), (0,0), (0,3). So c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nThus for b=2, c=1 only. For b=0, c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed pairs:\n(1,2)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(3,2)\n(0,1)\n(0,0)\n\nThus for each c, possible d values:\n\n- If c=0: allowed d such that (0,d) ∈ allowed: (0,1), (0,0). So d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- If c=1: allowed d such that (1,d) ∈ allowed: (1,2). So d=2.\n\n- If c=2: allowed d such that (2,d) ∈ allowed: (2,2), (2,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- If c=3: allowed d such that (3,d) ∈ allowed: (3,2). So d=2.\n\nThus for each c we have possible d.\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed pairs:\n(2,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,0)\n(1,3)\n(3,3)\n(0,1)\n\nThus for each d, possible a values such that (d,a) ∈ allowed.\n\nThus we need to find assignments (c,d) such that (b,c) allowed, (c,d) allowed, and (d,a) allowed for given a,b.\n\nThus we can enumerate for each (a,b) combo.\n\nList possible (a,b) combos:\n\n- a=3, b=2 (since b=2 possible)\n- a=3, b=0\n- a=0, b=2\n- a=0, b=0\n\nThus four combos.\n\nWe'll enumerate each.\n\n### Combo 1: a=3, b=2\n\nb=2 => c must be 1 (from c2). So c=1.\n\nNow c=1 => d must be 2 (from c3). So d=2.\n\nNow need (d,a) = (2,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (2,3). Yes (2,3) is allowed. So (c=1,d=2) works.\n\nThus for (a=3,b=2), we have exactly one solution: (c=1,d=2).\n\nThus C1 has 1 solution for (a=3,b=2).\n\n### Combo 2: a=3, b=0\n\nb=0 => c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nWe need to consider each possible c.\n\n- Subcase c=2: then d ∈ {2,1} (c3). So d can be 2 or 1.\n\n   - If d=2: need (d,a) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) allowed. So (c=2,d=2) works.\n\n   - If d=1: need (1,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (1,3). Yes (1,3) allowed. So (c=2,d=1) works.\n\n- Subcase c=0: then d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n   - If d=1: need (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) allowed. So (c=0,d=1) works.\n\n   - If d=0: need (0,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (0,1) but not (0,3). Let's check c4 allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So (0,3) is not allowed. So (c=0,d=0) fails.\n\n- Subcase c=3: then d=2 (c3). Need (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) allowed. So (c=3,d=2) works.\n\nThus for (a=3,b=0), we have solutions:\n\n- (c=2,d=2)\n- (c=2,d=1)\n- (c=0,d=1)\n- (c=3,d=2)\n\nThus 4 solutions.\n\nThus C1 has 4 solutions for (a=3,b=0).\n\n### Combo 3: a=0, b=2\n\nb=2 => c=1 (as before). c=1 => d=2. Need (d,a) = (2,0) allowed? c4 allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). (2,0) is not allowed. So (c=1,d=2) fails.\n\nThus no solution? Wait maybe there are other possibilities for c? But b=2 forces c=1 uniquely. So no solution for (a=0,b=2). So C1 has 0 solutions for (a=0,b=2).\n\nThus this combo is invalid.\n\n### Combo 4: a=0, b=0\n\nb=0 => c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nWe need to find (c,d) such that (c,d) allowed and (d,a) = (d,0) allowed.\n\nFirst, list allowed (d,a) pairs for a=0: need (d,0) ∈ allowed pairs for c4. Allowed pairs for c4: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So (d,0) allowed only if d=3? Actually (3,0) is allowed. Also maybe (0,0) not allowed. So only d=3 yields (3,0) allowed. So d must be 3.\n\nThus we need d=3.\n\nNow we need to find c such that (c,d) = (c,3) is allowed by c3 (c,d). c3 allowed pairs: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0). None have second entry 3. So there is no allowed (c,3). Thus no solution for (a=0,b=0). So C1 has 0 solutions for (a=0,b=0).\n\nThus C1 solutions exist only for combos (a=3,b=2) and (a=3,b=0). For (a=3,b=2) we have 1 solution; for (a=3,b=0) we have 4 solutions. So total solutions for C1 across all combos: 5 solutions.\n\nThus overall, the total number of full solutions for the entire CSP is sum over combos of (solutions for C1) * (solutions for C2) * (solutions for C3) * (solutions for C4). Since C4 always has 1 solution (o=3,p=2). So total solutions = sum_{combos} (C1_solutions * C2_solutions * C3_solutions). Where combos are defined by choices of a,b (2*2 combos), f (2 choices), j (2 choices). Actually combos are defined by (a,b) from C1, (e,f) from C2, (i,j) from C3, and (m,n) fixed. But note that (e,f) is linked to b via c17: e depends on b. So (e,f) combos are not independent of (a,b) because e is determined by b (b=2 => e=0; b=0 => e=2). So (e,f) combos are correlated with b. Similarly, (i) is determined by f (f=1 => i=0; f=2 => i=1). So (i,j) combos are correlated with f and j. So we need to consider the joint combos of (a,b,f,j) (4 combos for a,b; 2 combos for f; 2 combos for j) = 4*2*2 = 16 combos, as earlier.\n\nThus for each combination of (a,b,f,j), we can compute:\n\n- C1 solutions count based on (a,b) (we have counts: (a=3,b=2):1; (a=3,b=0):4; (a=0,b=2):0; (a=0,b=0):0).\n\n- C2 solutions count based on (e,f). e is determined by b: if b=2 => e=0; if b=0 => e=2. So for each (b,f) we can get C2 count.\n\n- C3 solutions count based on (i,j). i is determined by f: if f=1 => i=0; if f=2 => i=1. So for each (f,j) we can get C3 count.\n\nThus we can compute total solutions by summing over (a,b,f,j) combos: C1_count(a,b) * C2_count(b,f) * C3_count(f,j). Since C4 always 1.\n\nThus we need to compute C2_count for each (b,f) and C3_count for each (f,j). Then multiply with C1_count.\n\nThus we can compute the total number of solutions for the whole CSP.\n\nBut we only need the c4 tuple(s). Since C4 always yields (0,1,3,2), we need to check if any combos produce zero solutions for C1, C2, or C3. If a combo yields zero solutions for any of those, then that combo does not contribute any full solution. However, we only need to know if at least one full solution exists for the c4 tuple. Since C4 always yields that tuple, we need to see if there is at least one combo where all three subproblems have at least one solution. If yes, then that tuple is included.\n\nThus we need to find at least one combination (a,b,f,j) such that C1_count>0, C2_count>0, C3_count>0. Then the tuple is included.\n\nThus we need to find at least one valid combination.\n\nFrom C1 counts, only combos with (a=3,b=2) or (a=3,b=0) have non-zero solutions. So a must be 3. So a=3. So a must be 3. So a=3.\n\nThus a=3. Then b can be 2 or 0.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for (a,b): (3,2) and (3,0). So a=3, b∈{2,0}.\n\nNow for each b, e is determined: b=2 => e=0; b=0 => e=2.\n\nNow we need to consider f ∈ {1,2} and j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nNow we need C2_count for each (b,f). Let's compute C2_count for each combination of b and f.\n\nWe have C2_count table:\n\n- For (e=0,f=1): count=1\n- (e=0,f=2): count=1\n- (e=2,f=1): count=2\n- (e=2,f=2): count=2\n\nThus we need to map e to b: e=0 if b=2; e=2 if b=0.\n\nThus:\n\n- If b=2 (e=0):\n   - f=1 => C2_count = 1\n   - f=2 => C2_count = 1\n\n- If b=0 (e=2):\n   - f=1 => C2_count = 2\n   - f=2 => C2_count = 2\n\nThus C2_count depends only on f when b=2? Actually both f=1 and f=2 give count=1 when b=2. When b=0, both f=1 and f=2 give count=2.\n\nThus C2_count = 1 if b=2, else 2 if b=0 (independent of f). So C2_count = 1 if b=2; =2 if b=0.\n\nNow C3_count depends on (f,j). Let's compute C3_count for each (f,j) combination.\n\nWe enumerated C3 solutions earlier for (i,j) combos, where i depends on f: i=0 if f=1; i=1 if f=2. So we need to compute C3_count for each (f,j) pair.\n\nWe have C3 solutions counts:\n\n- (i=0,j=3): 2 solutions\n- (i=0,j=0): 2 solutions\n- (i=1,j=3): 0 solutions\n- (i=1,j=0): 1 solution\n\nThus mapping:\n\n- If f=1 (i=0):\n   - j=3 => C3_count = 2\n   - j=0 => C3_count = 2\n\n- If f=2 (i=1):\n   - j=3 => C3_count = 0\n   - j=0 => C3_count = 1\n\nThus C3_count = 2 if (f=1 and j ∈ {3,0}); C3_count = 0 if (f=2 and j=3); C3_count = 1 if (f=2 and j=0).\n\nThus we can compute total solutions for each combination (a,b,f,j) as:\n\nTotal = C1_count(a,b) * C2_count(b,f) * C3_count(f,j).\n\nSince C4 always 1.\n\nNow we need to find at least one combination where all three counts >0.\n\nLet's enumerate all combos:\n\nWe have a fixed to 3 (since only a=3 yields non-zero C1). So a=3.\n\nb ∈ {2,0}.\n\nf ∈ {1,2}.\n\nj ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus combos: (b,f,j) with b∈{2,0}, f∈{1,2}, j∈{3,0}.\n\nWe can compute for each.\n\nFirst, compute C1_count for each (a,b) with a=3:\n\n- (b=2): C1_count = 1 (we found earlier)\n- (b=0): C1_count = 4\n\nThus C1_count = 1 if b=2; =4 if b=0.\n\nNow C2_count:\n\n- If b=2: C2_count = 1 (for both f=1 or f=2)\n- If b=0: C2_count = 2 (for both f=1 or f=2)\n\nNow C3_count depends on f and j.\n\nThus we can enumerate:\n\nCase 1: b=2 (C1=1, C2=1). Then total = 1 * 1 * C3_count = C3_count.\n\nThus for b=2, we need C3_count >0. So we need (f,j) combos where C3_count >0.\n\n- f=1 (i=0):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=2\n   - j=0 => C3_count=2\n\n- f=2 (i=1):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=0 (invalid)\n   - j=0 => C3_count=1\n\nThus for b=2, valid combos are:\n\n- (f=1,j=3): total = 2\n- (f=1,j=0): total = 2\n- (f=2,j=0): total = 1\n\nThus there are solutions for b=2. So at least one full solution exists for b=2.\n\nThus we have at least one full solution for the whole CSP.\n\nNow case 2: b=0 (C1=4, C2=2). Then total = 4 * 2 * C3_count = 8 * C3_count.\n\nThus for b=0, we need C3_count >0.\n\n- f=1 (i=0):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=2 => total = 8*2 = 16\n   - j=0 => C3_count=2 => total = 16\n\n- f=2 (i=1):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=0 => total = 0 (invalid)\n   - j=0 => C3_count=1 => total = 8*1 = 8\n\nThus for b=0, valid combos: (f=1,j=3), (f=1,j=0), (f=2,j=0). All have non-zero solutions.\n\nThus many solutions exist.\n\nThus overall, there are many full solutions. So the c4 tuple (0,1,3,2) is definitely part of at least one full solution. So we need to output that tuple.\n\nThus the answer is a single tuple: [0,1,3,2] (ordered as m,n,o,p). However, we need to output the JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing that tuple. The envelope template shows a placeholder with one tuple [0,0,0,0]. We need to replace that with the correct tuple(s). Since we have exactly one tuple, we need to output an array with that tuple.\n\nThus the output JSON should be exactly the envelope template but with the \"tuples\" array replaced by [[0,1,3,2]].\n\nWe must ensure that the JSON is exactly as required: the envelope includes fields: intent, message_id, payload.claim.claim_id, etc. We must not modify anything else. The template includes \"payload.claim.value.tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ]. We need to replace that with [ [0,1,3,2] ].\n\nThus final output JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c4-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c4-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c4-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c4\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            1,\n            3,\n            2\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"m\",\n          \"n\",\n          \"o\",\n          \"p\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\":","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We have a CSP with variables a..p, domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints for pairs of variables (c1..c16) defined by allowed pairs. Additionally, there are \"bridges\" constraints c17..c20 that link variables across components: c17 is between c1 and c2 variables b,e; c18 between c2 and c3 variables f,i; c19 between c3 and c4 variables j,m; c20 between c4 and c1 variables n,a.\n\nWe need to \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c4, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nSo we need to find all assignments (m,n,o,p) that satisfy all constraints given the whole CSP. That includes constraints c13..c16 that involve m,n,o,p pairs directly, plus bridge constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). Also constraints c1..c12 involve other variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l. So we need to find all possible assignments for (m,n,o,p) that can be extended to a full solution for the entire CSP. So effectively we need to compute the projection of the solution set onto variables m,n,o,p (c4) given all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for component c4, i.e., all possible (m,n,o,p) that belong to some full solution of the CSP. Then output them sorted lexicographically (presumably by m,n,o,p in that order). The output must be JSON with the envelope format, but only editing the payload.claim.value.tuples part.\n\nThus we need to compute the solution set for the entire CSP, then extract the projection onto (m,n,o,p). The constraints are finite; domain size is 4; there are 16 variables, each with domain size 4. The constraints are binary constraints (allowed pairs). So we could brute force search: 4^16 = 4,294,967,296 possibilities, which is large but maybe manageable with pruning? However we can also reason logically.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible (m,n,o,p) that can be part of a full solution. It might be a relatively small set.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's try to approach systematically.\n\nWe have components:\n\nc1: a,b,c,d\nc2: e,f,g,h\nc3: i,j,k,l\nc4: m,n,o,p\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs:\n[\n[3,2],\n[2,3],\n[3,1],\n[0,2],\n[1,1],\n[0,1]\n]\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed:\n[\n[2,1],\n[0,2],\n[3,2],\n[0,0],\n[3,1],\n[0,3]\n]\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed:\n[\n[1,2],\n[2,2],\n[2,1],\n[3,2],\n[0,1],\n[0,0]\n]\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed:\n[\n[2,3],\n[2,2],\n[3,0],\n[1,3],\n[3,3],\n[0,1]\n]\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed:\n[\n[0,1],\n[1,1],\n[2,2],\n[0,2],\n[3,3],\n[2,1]\n]\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed:\n[\n[1,3],\n[2,3],\n[0,1],\n[2,0],\n[1,1],\n[1,0]\n]\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed:\n[\n[3,2],\n[3,1],\n[2,1],\n[1,0],\n[0,0],\n[3,0]\n]\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[1,1],\n[2,2],\n[2,1],\n[0,3],\n[1,2]\n]\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed:\n[\n[0,3],\n[0,2],\n[0,0],\n[1,1],\n[3,2],\n[2,3]\n]\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed:\n[\n[3,0],\n[0,2],\n[1,0],\n[0,1],\n[1,3],\n[3,2]\n]\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed:\n[\n[0,2],\n[1,3],\n[3,3],\n[2,0],\n[3,2],\n[1,0]\n]\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[1,1],\n[3,3],\n[2,2],\n[3,1],\n[0,0]\n]\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed:\n[\n[0,1],\n[2,2],\n[0,3],\n[3,0],\n[2,1],\n[2,0]\n]\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed:\n[\n[1,3],\n[3,2],\n[3,0],\n[0,1],\n[2,0],\n[0,3]\n]\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed:\n[\n[3,2],\n[0,2],\n[3,3],\n[0,1],\n[2,1],\n[1,1]\n]\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[3,3],\n[0,0],\n[1,2],\n[1,3],\n[2,3]\n]\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed:\n[\n[2,0],\n[0,2]\n]\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed:\n[\n[1,0],\n[2,1]\n]\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed:\n[\n[3,0],\n[0,0]\n]\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed:\n[\n[1,3],\n[1,0]\n]\n\nSo we have a CSP with 16 variables, binary constraints.\n\nWe need to find all possible assignments for (m,n,o,p). Approach: solve the CSP systematically, perhaps using constraint propagation or backtracking manually. Since domain size is small, we can attempt to enumerate possibilities for each component and combine.\n\nWe can treat each component as a chain: c1 (a,b,c,d) has constraints c1 (a,b), c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). That's a cycle: a->b->c->d->a. So it's a CSP on 4 variables with constraints forming a cycle.\n\nc2 (e,f,g,h) has constraints c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) - also a cycle.\n\nc3 (i,j,k,l) has constraints c9 (i,j), c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i) - also a cycle.\n\nc4 (m,n,o,p) has constraints c13 (m,n), c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m) - also a cycle.\n\nAdditionally, there are cross-component bridges: c17 (b,e), c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m), c20 (n,a). So we have connections linking the cycles.\n\nThus the whole CSP is a graph of 16 variables with cycles plus cross edges.\n\nWe need to find all solutions.\n\nWe could attempt to solve using backtracking manually. But there are many possibilities. However, maybe the constraints are restrictive enough that the number of solutions is small.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions for c4 (m,n,o,p). There may be many, but maybe not too many.\n\nLet's try to systematically solve.\n\nWe have 4 variables per component. Let's denote:\n\nComponent c1: a,b,c,d\nc2: e,f,g,h\nc3: i,j,k,l\nc4: m,n,o,p\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) from list A1\nc2: allowed (b,c) from list A2\nc3: allowed (c,d) from list A3\nc4: allowed (d,a) from list A4\n\nc5: allowed (e,f) from list A5\nc6: allowed (f,g) from list A6\nc7: allowed (g,h) from list A7\nc8: allowed (h,e) from list A8\n\nc9: allowed (i,j) from list A9\nc10: allowed (j,k) from list A10\nc11: allowed (k,l) from list A11\nc12: allowed (l,i) from list A12\n\nc13: allowed (m,n) from list A13\nc14: allowed (n,o) from list A14\nc15: allowed (o,p) from list A15\nc16: allowed (p,m) from list A16\n\nc17: allowed (b,e) from list A17\nc18: allowed (f,i) from list A18\nc19: allowed (j,m) from list A19\nc20: allowed (n,a) from list A20\n\nAll variables domain = {0,1,2,3}.\n\nGoal: find all assignments for (m,n,o,p) that can be extended to a full solution.\n\nWe need to consider constraints linking components: c17 links b and e; c18 links f and i; c19 links j and m; c20 links n and a.\n\nThus the cycles of each component are linked via these cross constraints, forming a larger structure.\n\nWe could try to solve the entire CSP using a systematic approach: treat the graph as having cycles; we could try to enumerate possibilities for one component at a time, propagate constraints.\n\nGiven the small domain, we could attempt to manually enumerate possibilities for each component using backtracking, perhaps using reasoning to prune.\n\nWe need all solutions for c4, so we might focus on solving for (m,n,o,p) while also considering constraints linking to other components, but we could also solve the entire CSP and then extract (m,n,o,p). The number of solutions might be manageable.\n\nWe need to be careful to enumerate all solutions. Let's try to systematically explore using constraint propagation.\n\nApproach: Since each component is a cycle of 4 variables, we can solve each component in isolation given the values of some variables. For example, c1 can be solved given a (or b). But there are cross constraints linking a (c20) to n, and b (c17) to e, etc. So we need to consider interactions.\n\nWe could treat the entire graph as a constraint satisfaction problem and attempt to enumerate all solutions using backtracking with pruning. Doing this manually is tedious, but we can try to reduce the search space by analyzing constraints.\n\nFirst, let's list the allowed pairs for each constraint for reference:\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed:\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n(3,1)\n(0,2)\n(1,1)\n(0,1)\n\nc2 (b,c):\n(2,1)\n(0,2)\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(3,1)\n(0,3)\n\nc3 (c,d):\n(1,2)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(3,2)\n(0,1)\n(0,0)\n\nc4 (d,a):\n(2,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,0)\n(1,3)\n(3,3)\n(0,1)\n\nc5 (e,f):\n(0,1)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(0,2)\n(3,3)\n(2,1)\n\nc6 (f,g):\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(1,0)\n\nc7 (g,h):\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,0)\n(0,0)\n(3,0)\n\nc8 (h,e):\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(0,3)\n(1,2)\n\nc9 (i,j):\n(0,3)\n(0,2)\n(0,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(2,3)\n\nc10 (j,k):\n(3,0)\n(0,2)\n(1,0)\n(0,1)\n(1,3)\n(3,2)\n\nc11 (k,l):\n(0,2)\n(1,3)\n(3,3)\n(2,0)\n(3,2)\n(1,0)\n\nc12 (l,i):\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(3,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,1)\n(0,0)\n\nc13 (m,n):\n(0,1)\n(2,2)\n(0,3)\n(3,0)\n(2,1)\n(2,0)\n\nc14 (n,o):\n(1,3)\n(3,2)\n(3,0)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(0,3)\n\nc15 (o,p):\n(3,2)\n(0,2)\n(3,3)\n(0,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,1)\n\nc16 (p,m):\n(2,0)\n(3,3)\n(0,0)\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n\nc17 (b,e):\n(2,0)\n(0,2)\n\nc18 (f,i):\n(1,0)\n(2,1)\n\nc19 (j,m):\n(3,0)\n(0,0)\n\nc20 (n,a):\n(1,3)\n(1,0)\n\nThus cross constraints are very restrictive: c17 only allows (b,e) = (2,0) or (0,2). c18 only allows (f,i) = (1,0) or (2,1). c19 only allows (j,m) = (3,0) or (0,0). c20 only allows (n,a) = (1,3) or (1,0).\n\nThus these cross constraints strongly restrict values of b, e; f,i; j,m; n,a.\n\nWe also have constraints c13..c16 linking m,n,o,p, and cross constraints c19 and c20 linking m to j and n to a. So m and n are heavily constrained.\n\nThus we can start by enumerating possibilities for cross constraints.\n\nLet’s list possibilities for each cross constraint:\n\nc17 (b,e):\n- Option 1: b=2, e=0\n- Option 2: b=0, e=2\n\nc18 (f,i):\n- Option A: f=1, i=0\n- Option B: f=2, i=1\n\nc19 (j,m):\n- Option α: j=3, m=0\n- Option β: j=0, m=0\n\nc20 (n,a):\n- Option X: n=1, a=3\n- Option Y: n=1, a=0\n\nThus we have 2*2*2*2 = 16 combinations of these cross constraints.\n\nNow we need to consider constraints within each component that involve these variables.\n\nComponent c1: variables a,b,c,d. Constraints: c1 (a,b), c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). Also cross constraints: c17 (b,e) and c20 (n,a). So a appears in c1 and c4 and c20; b appears in c1, c2, c17.\n\nComponent c2: variables e,f,g,h. Constraints: c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e). Cross constraints: c17 (b,e) and c18 (f,i). So e appears in c5, c8, c17; f appears in c5, c6, c18.\n\nComponent c3: variables i,j,k,l. Constraints: c9 (i,j), c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i). Cross constraints: c18 (f,i) and c19 (j,m). So i appears in c9, c12, c18; j appears in c9, c10, c19.\n\nComponent c4: variables m,n,o,p. Constraints: c13 (m,n), c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m). Cross constraints: c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). So m appears in c13, c16, c19; n appears in c13, c14, c20.\n\nThus the cross constraints tie the cycles together.\n\nWe need to find all solutions for the whole CSP. Let's attempt to solve using a systematic approach: we can treat the cross constraints as givens, then propagate within each component.\n\nGiven the cross constraints, we have values for some variables: b,e; f,i; j,m; n,a.\n\nThus we can treat those as known values for each combination, then solve each component's remaining variables.\n\nBut note that some of these variables also appear in other cross constraints: e appears only in c17 and c5,c8; b appears only in c17 and c1,c2; f appears only in c18 and c5,c6; i appears only in c18 and c9,c12; j appears only in c19 and c9,c10; m appears only in c19 and c13,c16; n appears only in c20 and c13,c14; a appears only in c20 and c1,c4.\n\nThus each variable appears in exactly one cross constraint (except maybe a appears only in c20 and c1,c4; b appears only in c17 and c1,c2; etc). So cross constraints essentially fix the values of those variables (b,e; f,i; j,m; n,a) to specific pairs.\n\nThus we can treat each cross constraint as a relation that restricts the values of those two variables.\n\nThus we need to consider all 16 combos of these cross constraints, then for each, solve the component cycles.\n\nWe need to find all solutions for the entire CSP. Then we extract (m,n,o,p) values.\n\nWe can attempt to enumerate all possibilities for (b,e) from c17, (f,i) from c18, (j,m) from c19, (n,a) from c20. Then for each combination, we need to find assignments for the remaining variables in each component: for c1: a,b,c,d; we know a from c20 and b from c17. So a and b are known. Then we need to find c,d such that constraints c2 (b,c) and c3 (c,d) and c4 (d,a) are satisfied. So given a and b, we need to find c,d.\n\nSimilarly for c2: e,f,g,h; we know e from c17 and f from c18. Then we need to find g,h satisfying c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e). So given e,f, find g,h.\n\nc3: i,j,k,l; we know i from c18 and j from c19. Then need to find k,l satisfying c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i). So given i,j, find k,l.\n\nc4: m,n,o,p; we know m from c19 and n from c20. Then need to find o,p satisfying c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m). So given m,n, find o,p.\n\nThus each component reduces to a smaller CSP: given two variables (the ones from cross constraints), find the remaining two variables.\n\nThus the total number of solutions is product of number of solutions for each component (if independent). However, there might be interactions? The cross constraints are independent across components: c17 ties b and e (c1 and c2), c18 ties f and i (c2 and c3), c19 ties j and m (c3 and c4), c20 ties n and a (c4 and c1). So there is a chain linking the components: c1-c2-c3-c4-c1. So the components are not independent because the cross constraints create connections across components. But we have already accounted for that: we fix values for b,e; f,i; j,m; n,a. Those are the cross constraints. The components themselves are independent given those values because each component's constraints only involve its own variables plus the cross constraints which we have fixed. There's no further cross constraints linking components beyond those four pairs. So after fixing those values, the components become independent: we can solve c1 given a,b; c2 given e,f; c3 given i,j; c4 given m,n. There is no further coupling.\n\nThus the total number of solutions is the product of the number of solutions for each component for each combination of cross constraint values.\n\nThus we can enumerate all combos of cross constraints (16 combos), and for each, count the number of solutions for each component. Then the total number of solutions is sum over combos of product of component solution counts.\n\nBut we only need the projection onto (m,n,o,p). So we need to collect all possible (m,n,o,p) values that appear in any solution.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all combos of cross constraints, and for each, enumerate all solutions for each component, then record (m,n,o,p) values.\n\nThus we need to solve each component subproblem for each possible pair values.\n\nThus we need to solve:\n\n- Subproblem C1: Given a and b (both from cross constraints), find all (c,d) such that constraints c2 (b,c) and c3 (c,d) and c4 (d,a) hold.\n\n- Subproblem C2: Given e and f, find all (g,h) such that constraints c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) hold.\n\n- Subproblem C3: Given i and j, find all (k,l) such that constraints c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i) hold.\n\n- Subproblem C4: Given m and n, find all (o,p) such that constraints c14 (n,o), c15 (o,p), c16 (p,m) hold.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possibilities for each subproblem given the specific values.\n\nThus we need to generate all possible assignments for a,b from cross constraints c20 and c17. Let's list possible combos:\n\nc20 (n,a): (n=1,a=3) or (n=1,a=0). So a ∈ {3,0}, n=1 always? Wait both options have n=1. Yes both have n=1. So n is fixed to 1 for all solutions. Interesting: c20 only allows n=1. So n=1 always. Then a is either 3 or 0.\n\nc17 (b,e): (b=2,e=0) or (b=0,e=2). So b ∈ {2,0}, e ∈ {0,2} correspondingly.\n\nc18 (f,i): (f=1,i=0) or (f=2,i=1). So f ∈ {1,2}, i ∈ {0,1} correspondingly.\n\nc19 (j,m): (j=3,m=0) or (j=0,m=0). So m=0 always! Both options have m=0. So m is fixed to 0. Then j is either 3 or 0.\n\nThus we have:\n\n- n = 1 always.\n- a ∈ {3,0}\n- b ∈ {2,0} (with e accordingly)\n- e = 0 if b=2 else e=2 if b=0 (since c17 pairs: (2,0) or (0,2))\n- f ∈ {1,2} (with i accordingly)\n- i = 0 if f=1 else i=1 if f=2\n- m = 0 always.\n- j ∈ {3,0} (depending on which option: if j=3,m=0; if j=0,m=0)\n- So j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus the cross constraints reduce the degrees of freedom significantly: we have choices for a (2 possibilities), b (2 possibilities), f (2 possibilities), j (2 possibilities). So total combos = 2*2*2*2 = 16 as expected.\n\nThus we need to consider all combos of (a ∈ {3,0}, b ∈ {2,0} with e accordingly, f ∈ {1,2} with i accordingly, j ∈ {3,0} with m=0). So we can enumerate all 16 combos.\n\nNow for each combo, we need to solve the component subproblems:\n\nC1: given a and b, find c,d.\n\nC2: given e and f, find g,h.\n\nC3: given i and j, find k,l.\n\nC4: given m=0 and n=1, find o,p.\n\nThus we need to enumerate solutions for each subproblem given the specific values.\n\nThus we can treat each subproblem as a small CSP with 2 variables (c,d) etc.\n\nWe can manually enumerate possibilities for each subproblem.\n\nLet's start with C4 because it's independent of many choices: given m=0, n=1, find o,p satisfying constraints c14 (n,o) i.e., (1,o) must be in allowed pairs for c14; c15 (o,p) must be allowed; c16 (p,m) i.e., (p,0) must be allowed.\n\nThus C4 is independent of other choices (since m and n are fixed). So we can solve C4 once: find all (o,p) such that:\n\n- (n=1, o) ∈ allowed pairs for c14 (n,o) => allowed pairs for c14 are: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So we need (1, o) to be one of those. So we need o such that (1,o) is in that list.\n\nList allowed (n,o) pairs: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So the first component is n; second is o. So we need n=1, so we need a pair where first entry is 1. Among the allowed pairs, only (1,3) has first entry 1. So (n=1, o=3) is allowed. No other pair has n=1. So o must be 3.\n\nThus o = 3 is forced.\n\nNow given o=3, we need to satisfy c15 (o,p): allowed pairs for c15 are: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So we need (o=3, p) to be one of these. So p must satisfy (3,p) ∈ allowed. The allowed pairs with first entry 3 are: (3,2), (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus p can be 2 or 3.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c16 (p,m): allowed pairs for c16 are: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So we need (p,m) = (p,0) to be allowed. So we need a pair where second entry is 0. Among allowed pairs, those with second entry 0 are: (2,0), (0,0). Also maybe others? Let's list all:\n\nc16 allowed pairs:\n- (2,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (2,3)\n\nThus only (2,0) and (0,0) have second entry 0. So p must be either 2 or 0.\n\nThus p ∈ {2,0}.\n\nCombine with p ∈ {2,3} from previous step, we get p must be 2 (common). So p=2.\n\nThus the only solution for C4 is (o=3, p=2). So (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2). So there is exactly one tuple for component c4 across all solutions! That seems plausible. But we must double-check if there are any other possibilities: Did we consider all allowed pairs for c14? Yes. Did we miss any pair where n=1? Only (1,3). So o forced to 3. Then p forced to 2. So indeed unique.\n\nThus the projection onto c4 is a single tuple: (m=0, n=1, o=3, p=2). So answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify that this tuple indeed satisfies all constraints, including cross constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a). Since m=0, c19 allows (j,m) = (j,0) must be either (3,0) or (0,0). So j can be 3 or 0. So both are allowed. So j is free (3 or 0). Similarly, n=1, c20 allows (n,a) = (1,a) must be either (1,3) or (1,0). So a can be 3 or 0. So a is free (3 or 0). So indeed (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2) is consistent with cross constraints.\n\nThus the answer for c4 is a single tuple.\n\nBut we must ensure that there are no other solutions for c4 that could arise if we consider other combos of cross constraints? But we saw that m and n are fixed by cross constraints: m=0 always, n=1 always. So any solution must have those values. Then we solved C4 subproblem for those values and found a unique solution (o=3,p=2). So indeed there is exactly one possible tuple for c4.\n\nThus the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify that the constraints for c4 (c13..c16) are satisfied for (m=0,n=1,o=3,p=2). Let's check:\n\nc13 (m,n) = (0,1) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for c13: (0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0). So (0,1) is allowed. Good.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (1,3) allowed? Allowed pairs for c14: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So (1,3) is allowed.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,2) allowed? Allowed pairs for c15: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So (3,2) is allowed.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (2,0) allowed? Allowed pairs for c16: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So (2,0) is allowed.\n\nThus c4 constraints satisfied.\n\nThus indeed (0,1,3,2) is a valid tuple for component c4.\n\nNow we need to verify that there are no other possible (o,p) combos for given (m=0,n=1). We solved C4 and found only (o=3,p=2). Let's double-check if there might be any alternative for o from c14: we only considered (1,3) because n=1. But is there any other allowed pair where first entry is 1? Let's list c14 allowed pairs again: \n\nc14 allowed pairs:\n[\n[1,3],\n[3,2],\n[3,0],\n[0,1],\n[2,0],\n[0,3]\n]\n\nThus first entries: 1,3,3,0,2,0. So only (1,3) has first entry 1. So o=3 forced.\n\nThus o is forced.\n\nNow for c15: allowed pairs list:\n[\n[3,2],\n[0,2],\n[3,3],\n[0,1],\n[2,1],\n[1,1]\n]\n\nThus for o=3, allowed p: (3,2) and (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c16: allowed pairs:\n[\n[2,0],\n[3,3],\n[0,0],\n[1,2],\n[1,3],\n[2,3]\n]\n\nThus for p=2, allowed m: (2,0) is allowed (since m=0). So p=2 works. For p=3, allowed m: (3,3) is allowed only if m=3, but m=0, so p=3 not allowed. Also (1,3) has m=3? No, (1,3) means (p,m) = (1,3) so p=1,m=3, not relevant. (2,3) means p=2,m=3, not relevant. So p=3 not allowed. So p must be 2. So indeed unique.\n\nThus C4 has exactly one solution.\n\nThus the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify that the other components can be solved for any combination of cross constraints (a,b,f,j). However, we don't need to enumerate those because we only need c4 tuples. However, we need to ensure that there is at least one full solution for each cross constraint combination that yields the c4 tuple. If some cross constraint combos lead to inconsistency in other components, then the c4 tuple may not be realizable. But we need to find all (m,n,o,p) that can be part of a full solution. Since c4 tuple is unique, we need to check if there exists at least one full solution consistent with that tuple. If there is at least one full solution, then that tuple is included. If there is none, then there are no solutions for c4 (but we found a solution for c4 constraints alone, but we need to ensure that other components can be satisfied with the cross constraints linking to m,n). So we need to check if there exists at least one assignment for the other variables (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l) that satisfies all constraints given the cross constraints and the c4 tuple.\n\nThus we need to find at least one full solution for the entire CSP that includes (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2). Since we have cross constraints linking m to j and n to a, we need to assign j and a accordingly: j ∈ {3,0} (since (j,m) = (j,0) must be either (3,0) or (0,0)). So j can be 3 or 0. Similarly, a ∈ {3,0} (since (n,a) = (1,a) must be either (1,3) or (1,0)). So a can be 3 or 0.\n\nThus we have two choices for j and two choices for a. Combined with choices for b (2) and f (2). So total 16 combos.\n\nNow we need to see if for each combination we can find assignments for the remaining variables in each component (c1,c2,c3,c4). Since c4 is fixed, we need to solve c1 given a,b; c2 given e,f; c3 given i,j; c4 already solved.\n\nThus we need to check if for each combination of a,b,e,f,i,j (subject to cross constraints), there exist solutions for c1,c2,c3. If any combination yields no solution for at least one component, then that combination is invalid, but the c4 tuple may still be realizable via other combos. So we need to find at least one combo that yields a full solution. If there is at least one, then the tuple is included.\n\nThus we need to check existence of solutions for c1,c2,c3 for some combos.\n\nThus we need to solve subproblems:\n\nC1: given a ∈ {3,0} and b ∈ {2,0} (with e accordingly: e = 0 if b=2 else e=2 if b=0), find c,d such that constraints c2 (b,c) and c3 (c,d) and c4 (d,a) hold.\n\nC2: given e ∈ {0,2} and f ∈ {1,2} (with i accordingly: i = 0 if f=1 else i=1 if f=2), find g,h such that constraints c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e) hold.\n\nC3: given i ∈ {0,1} and j ∈ {3,0} (with m=0 fixed), find k,l such that constraints c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i) hold.\n\nThus we need to find at least one combination of (a,b,f,j) that yields solutions for all three subproblems.\n\nThus we need to enumerate possibilities for each subproblem.\n\nGiven the small domain, we can manually enumerate possibilities for each subproblem.\n\nLet's start with C3 (i,j) subproblem. This is independent of other components. So we can enumerate all possible (i,j) pairs from cross constraints: i ∈ {0,1} (depending on f), j ∈ {3,0} (depending on which option). So there are 2*2 = 4 combos for (i,j). For each, we need to find (k,l) satisfying c10 (j,k), c11 (k,l), c12 (l,i). Let's enumerate all possibilities for each (i,j).\n\nFirst, list allowed pairs for c10 (j,k): allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for each j, possible k values:\n\n- If j=3: allowed k such that (3,k) ∈ allowed. Pairs with j=3: (3,0) and (3,2). So k ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- If j=0: allowed k such that (0,k) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,2), (0,1). Also maybe (0,?) from other pairs? Let's list all c10 pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for j=0, we have (0,2) and (0,1). So k ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus for j=3, k ∈ {0,2}; for j=0, k ∈ {2,1}.\n\nNow c11 (k,l): allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So for each k, possible l values:\n\n- If k=0: allowed l such that (0,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,2). So l=2.\n\n- If k=1: allowed l such that (1,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 1: (1,3), (1,0). So l ∈ {3,0}.\n\n- If k=2: allowed l such that (2,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,0). So l=0.\n\n- If k=3: allowed l such that (3,l) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 3: (3,3), (3,2). So l ∈ {3,2}.\n\nThus for each k, l is determined (or limited).\n\nNow c12 (l,i): allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,2), (3,1), (0,0). So for each l, possible i values such that (l,i) ∈ allowed.\n\nThus we need to find assignments (k,l) such that for given i, (l,i) is allowed.\n\nThus we can enumerate all possibilities for each (i,j) combo.\n\nLet's enumerate all combos for (i,j):\n\nCase 1: i=0, j=3\nCase 2: i=0, j=0\nCase 3: i=1, j=3\nCase 4: i=1, j=0\n\nWe'll enumerate for each.\n\n### Case 1: i=0, j=3\n\nj=3 => k ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- Subcase k=0: then l must be 2 (from c11). Then we need (l,i) = (2,0) allowed? c12 allowed pairs include (2,0). Yes (2,0) is allowed. So (k=0,l=2) works.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l must be 0 (from c11). Then need (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? c12 includes (0,0). Yes. So (k=2,l=0) works.\n\nThus for (i=0,j=3), we have two solutions: (k,l) = (0,2) and (2,0).\n\nThus C3 has solutions for (i=0,j=3).\n\n### Case 2: i=0, j=0\n\nj=0 => k ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l=0 (c11). Need (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? Yes. So (k=2,l=0) works.\n\n- Subcase k=1: then l ∈ {3,0} (c11). Need (l,i) = (l,0) allowed? Check c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,2), (3,1), (0,0). So (l,0) allowed only if l=2? Actually (2,0) is allowed, (0,0) is allowed. So l must be 0 or 2. But for k=1, l ∈ {3,0}. So l=0 works (since (0,0) allowed). l=3 does not work because (3,0) is not in c12 allowed pairs (allowed pairs with l=3 are (3,3) and (3,1)). So only l=0 works.\n\nThus for (i=0,j=0), we have solutions: (k=2,l=0) and (k=1,l=0). So two solutions.\n\nThus C3 has solutions for (i=0,j=0).\n\n### Case 3: i=1, j=3\n\nj=3 => k ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- Subcase k=0: then l=2 (c11). Need (l,i) = (2,1) allowed? c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,2), (3,1), (0,0). (2,1) is not allowed. So k=0 fails.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l=0 (c11). Need (l,i) = (0,1) allowed? c12 allowed pairs: (0,0) only for l=0? Actually (0,0) is allowed, (0,?) others? Only (0,0) appears. So (0,1) not allowed. So k=2 fails.\n\nThus no solutions for (i=1,j=3). So C3 has no solutions for (i=1,j=3).\n\n### Case 4: i=1, j=0\n\nj=0 => k ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase k=2: then l=0 (c11). Need (l,i) = (0,1) allowed? No, (0,1) not allowed. So k=2 fails.\n\n- Subcase k=1: then l ∈ {3,0} (c11). Need (l,i) = (l,1) allowed? c12 allowed pairs: (1,1) is allowed (l=1,i=1). But l ∈ {3,0}. So (3,1) is allowed? Yes, c12 includes (3,1). So l=3 works: (3,1) allowed. Also (0,1) is not allowed. So l=3 works.\n\nThus for (i=1,j=0), we have solution (k=1,l=3). Also check if l=0 works? (0,1) not allowed. So only (k=1,l=3) works.\n\nThus C3 has solutions for (i=1,j=0) (one solution).\n\nThus summary of C3 solutions:\n\n- (i=0,j=3): (k,l) = (0,2) or (2,0) (2 solutions)\n- (i=0,j=0): (k,l) = (2,0) or (1,0) (2 solutions)\n- (i=1,j=3): none\n- (i=1,j=0): (k,l) = (1,3) (1 solution)\n\nThus for each possible (i,j) there are solutions except (i=1,j=3) which has none.\n\nThus the cross constraints for i and j must not be (i=1,j=3). That corresponds to f=2 (i=1) and j=3 (i.e., j=3). So if we choose f=2 (i=1) and j=3, then C3 has no solution, making that combination invalid. So we must avoid that combination.\n\nThus we have 4 combos for (i,j). Among them, 3 combos are viable (i=0,j=3; i=0,j=0; i=1,j=0). The combo (i=1,j=3) is invalid.\n\nThus the cross constraints must avoid f=2 and j=3 simultaneously. So either f=1 (i=0) or j=0 (or both). So we need to ensure at least one valid combination.\n\nNow we need to consider C2 (e,f) subproblem. e ∈ {0,2} depending on b; f ∈ {1,2} depending on choice. So we have 2*2 = 4 combos for (e,f). For each, we need to find (g,h) satisfying c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e). Let's enumerate all possibilities for each (e,f) pair.\n\nList allowed pairs for each constraint:\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed pairs:\n(1,3)\n(2,3)\n(0,1)\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(1,0)\n\nThus for each f, possible g values:\n\n- If f=1: allowed g such that (1,g) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 1: (1,3), (1,1), (1,0). So g ∈ {3,1,0}.\n\n- If f=2: allowed g such that (2,g) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,3), (2,0). So g ∈ {3,0}.\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed pairs:\n(3,2)\n(3,1)\n(2,1)\n(1,0)\n(0,0)\n(3,0)\n\nThus for each g, possible h values:\n\n- If g=0: allowed h such that (0,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,0). So h=0.\n\n- If g=1: allowed h such that (1,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 1: (1,0). So h=0.\n\n- If g=2: allowed h such that (2,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,1). So h=1.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h such that (3,h) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 3: (3,2), (3,1), (3,0). So h ∈ {2,1,0}.\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed pairs:\n(2,0)\n(1,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(0,3)\n(1,2)\n\nThus for each h, possible e values such that (h,e) ∈ allowed.\n\nThus we need to find (g,h) such that:\n\n- g ∈ allowed for given f.\n- h ∈ allowed for given g.\n- (h,e) ∈ allowed for given e.\n\nThus we can enumerate for each (e,f) combo.\n\nList possible (e,f) combos based on cross constraints:\n\nc17: (b,e) pairs: (2,0) or (0,2). So e ∈ {0,2}. b ∈ {2,0} accordingly.\n\nc18: (f,i) pairs: (1,0) or (2,1). So f ∈ {1,2}. i accordingly.\n\nThus (e,f) combos: e=0 with f=1; e=0 with f=2; e=2 with f=1; e=2 with f=2. So four combos.\n\nThus we need to solve C2 for each of these four combos.\n\nLet's enumerate each case:\n\n#### Case A: e=0, f=1\n\n- f=1 => g ∈ {3,1,0}\n- For each g, find h:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=1 => h=0\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Then need (h,e) = (h,0) allowed? c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So (h,0) allowed only if h=2? Actually (2,0) is allowed. Also maybe (0,0) not allowed. So only h=2 yields (2,0). So h must be 2.\n\nThus we need h=2. So we need to find g such that h=2 is allowed.\n\n- g=3 => h can be 2,1,0. So h=2 possible. So g=3 works (with h=2).\n- g=1 => h=0 only, not 2. So g=1 fails.\n- g=0 => h=0 only, fails.\n\nThus only solution: g=3, h=2.\n\nThus for (e=0,f=1), we have a unique solution (g=3,h=2). Check constraints: c6 (f=1,g=3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is allowed. c7 (g=3,h=2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is allowed. c8 (h=2,e=0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is allowed. So valid.\n\nThus C2 has exactly one solution for (e=0,f=1).\n\n#### Case B: e=0, f=2\n\n- f=2 => g ∈ {3,0}\n- For each g:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Need (h,e) = (h,0) allowed? As before, only h=2 allowed (since (2,0) allowed). So h must be 2.\n\nThus we need h=2. So we need a g that allows h=2.\n\n- g=3 => h can be 2,1,0. So h=2 possible. So g=3 works (with h=2).\n- g=0 => h=0 only, fails.\n\nThus solution: g=3, h=2.\n\nThus for (e=0,f=2), we have solution (g=3,h=2). Check constraints: c6 (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is allowed. c7 (3,2) allowed? Yes. c8 (2,0) allowed? Yes. So valid.\n\nThus C2 has exactly one solution for (e=0,f=2).\n\n#### Case C: e=2, f=1\n\n- f=1 => g ∈ {3,1,0}\n- For each g:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=1 => h=0\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Need (h,e) = (h,2) allowed? c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So (h,2) allowed if h=1? Actually (1,2) is allowed. Also (2,2) is allowed (h=2). Also maybe (0,2) not allowed. (2,2) is allowed. (1,2) is allowed. So h ∈ {1,2} yields (h,2) allowed.\n\nThus we need h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow find g such that h can be 1 or 2.\n\n- g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}. So h can be 2 or 1. So g=3 works (choose h=2 or h=1). So there are two possible h values: h=2 or h=1.\n\n- g=1 => h=0 only, not allowed.\n\n- g=0 => h=0 only, not allowed.\n\nThus possible solutions: (g=3, h=2) or (g=3, h=1). Both satisfy constraints? Let's check each:\n\n- (g=3, h=2): c6 (1,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,2) allowed? Yes. c8 (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is allowed.\n\n- (g=3, h=1): c6 (1,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) allowed. c8 (1,2) allowed? Yes (1,2) allowed.\n\nThus both are valid. So C2 has two solutions for (e=2,f=1): (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1).\n\nThus count = 2.\n\n#### Case D: e=2, f=2\n\n- f=2 => g ∈ {3,0}\n- For each g:\n  - g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}\n  - g=0 => h=0\n\n- Need (h,e) = (h,2) allowed? As before, h ∈ {1,2} allowed.\n\nThus we need h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nCheck possibilities:\n\n- g=3 => h ∈ {2,1,0}. So h can be 2 or 1. So g=3 works with h=2 or h=1.\n\n- g=0 => h=0 only, not allowed.\n\nThus solutions: (g=3, h=2) and (g=3, h=1). Both satisfy constraints? Check:\n\n- (g=3, h=2): c6 (2,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,2) allowed? Yes. c8 (2,2) allowed? Yes.\n\n- (g=3, h=1): c6 (2,3) allowed? Yes. c7 (3,1) allowed? Yes. c8 (1,2) allowed? Yes.\n\nThus C2 has two solutions for (e=2,f=2): (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1).\n\nThus summary for C2:\n\n- (e=0,f=1): 1 solution: (g=3,h=2)\n- (e=0,f=2): 1 solution: (g=3,h=2)\n- (e=2,f=1): 2 solutions: (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1)\n- (e=2,f=2): 2 solutions: (g=3,h=2) and (g=3,h=1)\n\nThus total solutions for C2 across all combos: 1+1+2+2 = 6 solutions.\n\nNow we need to consider C1 (a,b) subproblem. a ∈ {3,0} (depending on cross constraint c20). b ∈ {2,0} (depending on cross constraint c17). So we have 2*2 = 4 combos for (a,b). For each, we need to find (c,d) satisfying constraints c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). Let's enumerate.\n\nList allowed pairs for each constraint:\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed pairs:\n(2,1)\n(0,2)\n(3,2)\n(0,0)\n(3,1)\n(0,3)\n\nThus for each b, possible c values:\n\n- If b=2: allowed c such that (2,c) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 2: (2,1). So c=1.\n\n- If b=0: allowed c such that (0,c) ∈ allowed. Pairs with first entry 0: (0,2), (0,0), (0,3). So c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nThus for b=2, c=1 only. For b=0, c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed pairs:\n(1,2)\n(2,2)\n(2,1)\n(3,2)\n(0,1)\n(0,0)\n\nThus for each c, possible d values:\n\n- If c=0: allowed d such that (0,d) ∈ allowed: (0,1), (0,0). So d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- If c=1: allowed d such that (1,d) ∈ allowed: (1,2). So d=2.\n\n- If c=2: allowed d such that (2,d) ∈ allowed: (2,2), (2,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- If c=3: allowed d such that (3,d) ∈ allowed: (3,2). So d=2.\n\nThus for each c we have possible d.\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed pairs:\n(2,3)\n(2,2)\n(3,0)\n(1,3)\n(3,3)\n(0,1)\n\nThus for each d, possible a values such that (d,a) ∈ allowed.\n\nThus we need to find assignments (c,d) such that (b,c) allowed, (c,d) allowed, and (d,a) allowed for given a,b.\n\nThus we can enumerate for each (a,b) combo.\n\nList possible (a,b) combos:\n\n- a=3, b=2 (since b=2 possible)\n- a=3, b=0\n- a=0, b=2\n- a=0, b=0\n\nThus four combos.\n\nWe'll enumerate each.\n\n### Combo 1: a=3, b=2\n\nb=2 => c must be 1 (from c2). So c=1.\n\nNow c=1 => d must be 2 (from c3). So d=2.\n\nNow need (d,a) = (2,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (2,3). Yes (2,3) is allowed. So (c=1,d=2) works.\n\nThus for (a=3,b=2), we have exactly one solution: (c=1,d=2).\n\nThus C1 has 1 solution for (a=3,b=2).\n\n### Combo 2: a=3, b=0\n\nb=0 => c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nWe need to consider each possible c.\n\n- Subcase c=2: then d ∈ {2,1} (c3). So d can be 2 or 1.\n\n   - If d=2: need (d,a) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) allowed. So (c=2,d=2) works.\n\n   - If d=1: need (1,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (1,3). Yes (1,3) allowed. So (c=2,d=1) works.\n\n- Subcase c=0: then d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n   - If d=1: need (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) allowed. So (c=0,d=1) works.\n\n   - If d=0: need (0,3) allowed? c4 allowed pairs include (0,1) but not (0,3). Let's check c4 allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So (0,3) is not allowed. So (c=0,d=0) fails.\n\n- Subcase c=3: then d=2 (c3). Need (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) allowed. So (c=3,d=2) works.\n\nThus for (a=3,b=0), we have solutions:\n\n- (c=2,d=2)\n- (c=2,d=1)\n- (c=0,d=1)\n- (c=3,d=2)\n\nThus 4 solutions.\n\nThus C1 has 4 solutions for (a=3,b=0).\n\n### Combo 3: a=0, b=2\n\nb=2 => c=1 (as before). c=1 => d=2. Need (d,a) = (2,0) allowed? c4 allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). (2,0) is not allowed. So (c=1,d=2) fails.\n\nThus no solution? Wait maybe there are other possibilities for c? But b=2 forces c=1 uniquely. So no solution for (a=0,b=2). So C1 has 0 solutions for (a=0,b=2).\n\nThus this combo is invalid.\n\n### Combo 4: a=0, b=0\n\nb=0 => c ∈ {2,0,3}.\n\nWe need to find (c,d) such that (c,d) allowed and (d,a) = (d,0) allowed.\n\nFirst, list allowed (d,a) pairs for a=0: need (d,0) ∈ allowed pairs for c4. Allowed pairs for c4: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So (d,0) allowed only if d=3? Actually (3,0) is allowed. Also maybe (0,0) not allowed. So only d=3 yields (3,0) allowed. So d must be 3.\n\nThus we need d=3.\n\nNow we need to find c such that (c,d) = (c,3) is allowed by c3 (c,d). c3 allowed pairs: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0). None have second entry 3. So there is no allowed (c,3). Thus no solution for (a=0,b=0). So C1 has 0 solutions for (a=0,b=0).\n\nThus C1 solutions exist only for combos (a=3,b=2) and (a=3,b=0). For (a=3,b=2) we have 1 solution; for (a=3,b=0) we have 4 solutions. So total solutions for C1 across all combos: 5 solutions.\n\nThus overall, the total number of full solutions for the entire CSP is sum over combos of (solutions for C1) * (solutions for C2) * (solutions for C3) * (solutions for C4). Since C4 always has 1 solution (o=3,p=2). So total solutions = sum_{combos} (C1_solutions * C2_solutions * C3_solutions). Where combos are defined by choices of a,b (2*2 combos), f (2 choices), j (2 choices). Actually combos are defined by (a,b) from C1, (e,f) from C2, (i,j) from C3, and (m,n) fixed. But note that (e,f) is linked to b via c17: e depends on b. So (e,f) combos are not independent of (a,b) because e is determined by b (b=2 => e=0; b=0 => e=2). So (e,f) combos are correlated with b. Similarly, (i) is determined by f (f=1 => i=0; f=2 => i=1). So (i,j) combos are correlated with f and j. So we need to consider the joint combos of (a,b,f,j) (4 combos for a,b; 2 combos for f; 2 combos for j) = 4*2*2 = 16 combos, as earlier.\n\nThus for each combination of (a,b,f,j), we can compute:\n\n- C1 solutions count based on (a,b) (we have counts: (a=3,b=2):1; (a=3,b=0):4; (a=0,b=2):0; (a=0,b=0):0).\n\n- C2 solutions count based on (e,f). e is determined by b: if b=2 => e=0; if b=0 => e=2. So for each (b,f) we can get C2 count.\n\n- C3 solutions count based on (i,j). i is determined by f: if f=1 => i=0; if f=2 => i=1. So for each (f,j) we can get C3 count.\n\nThus we can compute total solutions by summing over (a,b,f,j) combos: C1_count(a,b) * C2_count(b,f) * C3_count(f,j). Since C4 always 1.\n\nThus we need to compute C2_count for each (b,f) and C3_count for each (f,j). Then multiply with C1_count.\n\nThus we can compute the total number of solutions for the whole CSP.\n\nBut we only need the c4 tuple(s). Since C4 always yields (0,1,3,2), we need to check if any combos produce zero solutions for C1, C2, or C3. If a combo yields zero solutions for any of those, then that combo does not contribute any full solution. However, we only need to know if at least one full solution exists for the c4 tuple. Since C4 always yields that tuple, we need to see if there is at least one combo where all three subproblems have at least one solution. If yes, then that tuple is included.\n\nThus we need to find at least one combination (a,b,f,j) such that C1_count>0, C2_count>0, C3_count>0. Then the tuple is included.\n\nThus we need to find at least one valid combination.\n\nFrom C1 counts, only combos with (a=3,b=2) or (a=3,b=0) have non-zero solutions. So a must be 3. So a=3. So a must be 3. So a=3.\n\nThus a=3. Then b can be 2 or 0.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for (a,b): (3,2) and (3,0). So a=3, b∈{2,0}.\n\nNow for each b, e is determined: b=2 => e=0; b=0 => e=2.\n\nNow we need to consider f ∈ {1,2} and j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nNow we need C2_count for each (b,f). Let's compute C2_count for each combination of b and f.\n\nWe have C2_count table:\n\n- For (e=0,f=1): count=1\n- (e=0,f=2): count=1\n- (e=2,f=1): count=2\n- (e=2,f=2): count=2\n\nThus we need to map e to b: e=0 if b=2; e=2 if b=0.\n\nThus:\n\n- If b=2 (e=0):\n   - f=1 => C2_count = 1\n   - f=2 => C2_count = 1\n\n- If b=0 (e=2):\n   - f=1 => C2_count = 2\n   - f=2 => C2_count = 2\n\nThus C2_count depends only on f when b=2? Actually both f=1 and f=2 give count=1 when b=2. When b=0, both f=1 and f=2 give count=2.\n\nThus C2_count = 1 if b=2, else 2 if b=0 (independent of f). So C2_count = 1 if b=2; =2 if b=0.\n\nNow C3_count depends on (f,j). Let's compute C3_count for each (f,j) combination.\n\nWe enumerated C3 solutions earlier for (i,j) combos, where i depends on f: i=0 if f=1; i=1 if f=2. So we need to compute C3_count for each (f,j) pair.\n\nWe have C3 solutions counts:\n\n- (i=0,j=3): 2 solutions\n- (i=0,j=0): 2 solutions\n- (i=1,j=3): 0 solutions\n- (i=1,j=0): 1 solution\n\nThus mapping:\n\n- If f=1 (i=0):\n   - j=3 => C3_count = 2\n   - j=0 => C3_count = 2\n\n- If f=2 (i=1):\n   - j=3 => C3_count = 0\n   - j=0 => C3_count = 1\n\nThus C3_count = 2 if (f=1 and j ∈ {3,0}); C3_count = 0 if (f=2 and j=3); C3_count = 1 if (f=2 and j=0).\n\nThus we can compute total solutions for each combination (a,b,f,j) as:\n\nTotal = C1_count(a,b) * C2_count(b,f) * C3_count(f,j).\n\nSince C4 always 1.\n\nNow we need to find at least one combination where all three counts >0.\n\nLet's enumerate all combos:\n\nWe have a fixed to 3 (since only a=3 yields non-zero C1). So a=3.\n\nb ∈ {2,0}.\n\nf ∈ {1,2}.\n\nj ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus combos: (b,f,j) with b∈{2,0}, f∈{1,2}, j∈{3,0}.\n\nWe can compute for each.\n\nFirst, compute C1_count for each (a,b) with a=3:\n\n- (b=2): C1_count = 1 (we found earlier)\n- (b=0): C1_count = 4\n\nThus C1_count = 1 if b=2; =4 if b=0.\n\nNow C2_count:\n\n- If b=2: C2_count = 1 (for both f=1 or f=2)\n- If b=0: C2_count = 2 (for both f=1 or f=2)\n\nNow C3_count depends on f and j.\n\nThus we can enumerate:\n\nCase 1: b=2 (C1=1, C2=1). Then total = 1 * 1 * C3_count = C3_count.\n\nThus for b=2, we need C3_count >0. So we need (f,j) combos where C3_count >0.\n\n- f=1 (i=0):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=2\n   - j=0 => C3_count=2\n\n- f=2 (i=1):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=0 (invalid)\n   - j=0 => C3_count=1\n\nThus for b=2, valid combos are:\n\n- (f=1,j=3): total = 2\n- (f=1,j=0): total = 2\n- (f=2,j=0): total = 1\n\nThus there are solutions for b=2. So at least one full solution exists for b=2.\n\nThus we have at least one full solution for the whole CSP.\n\nNow case 2: b=0 (C1=4, C2=2). Then total = 4 * 2 * C3_count = 8 * C3_count.\n\nThus for b=0, we need C3_count >0.\n\n- f=1 (i=0):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=2 => total = 8*2 = 16\n   - j=0 => C3_count=2 => total = 16\n\n- f=2 (i=1):\n   - j=3 => C3_count=0 => total = 0 (invalid)\n   - j=0 => C3_count=1 => total = 8*1 = 8\n\nThus for b=0, valid combos: (f=1,j=3), (f=1,j=0), (f=2,j=0). All have non-zero solutions.\n\nThus many solutions exist.\n\nThus overall, there are many full solutions. So the c4 tuple (0,1,3,2) is definitely part of at least one full solution. So we need to output that tuple.\n\nThus the answer is a single tuple: [0,1,3,2] (ordered as m,n,o,p). However, we need to output the JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing that tuple. The envelope template shows a placeholder with one tuple [0,0,0,0]. We need to replace that with the correct tuple(s). Since we have exactly one tuple, we need to output an array with that tuple.\n\nThus the output JSON should be exactly the envelope template but with the \"tuples\" array replaced by [[0,1,3,2]].\n\nWe must ensure that the JSON is exactly as required: the envelope includes fields: intent, message_id, payload.claim.claim_id, etc. We must not modify anything else. The template includes \"payload.claim.value.tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ]. We need to replace that with [ [0,1,3,2] ].\n\nThus final output JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c4-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c4-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c4-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c4\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            1,\n            3,\n            2\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"m\",\n          \"n\",\n          \"o\",\n          \"p\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\":","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2679,"completion_tokens":16384,"total_tokens":19063,"cost":0,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":11656,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}